# The upload key for Google Play — the one file in this arrangement that must
# never be committed.
#
# Copy this file to `android/keystore.properties`, keep the keystore somewhere
# outside the repository, and fill in the values from the key you created:
#
#   keytool -genkeypair -v -keystore /secure/upload-keystore.jks \
#     -alias upload -keyalg RSA -keysize 4096 -validity 10000
#
# Both this file and the keystore are ignored by git (see the signing section at
# the foot of the root `.gitignore`), and `npm run android:preflight` fails if
# either of them has ever been tracked.
#
# What this key is, and what it is not. With Play App Signing — which every new
# application uses — Google holds the key that actually signs what a phone
# installs, and this is only the *upload* key: the one that proves to Play who
# sent the bundle. Losing this one is recoverable (Play Console can reset an
# upload key); losing the app signing key is not, which is precisely why Play
# keeps that one and the repository keeps this one.
#
# `storeFile` is resolved relative to `android/app/`, because the Gradle module
# that reads it lives there.
#
# On Windows, write a path with forward slashes:
#
#   storeFile=C:/Users/you/keys/se27/upload-keystore.jks
#
# Gradle loads this file with `java.util.Properties`, where a backslash is an
# escape: `\k` and `\s` lose their backslash and `\u` in `\upload-keystore.jks`
# is a malformed Unicode escape, so `load` throws `Malformed \uxxxx encoding`
# before Gradle configures anything. Doubling every backslash works too, since
# that is Java's way of writing one. Avoid a backslash in a password for the
# same reason — `pa\ss` is read back as `pass`.
#
# `keystore.properties.example` itself contains no secret and is committed.
storeFile=upload-keystore.jks
storePassword=
keyAlias=upload
keyPassword=
